{"name":"Bushel","hex":"#9fa993","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bushel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dry measure, containing four pecks, eight gallons, or thirty-two quarts; equivalent in volume to approximately 0.0364 cubic meters (imperial bushel) or 0.0352 cubic meters (U.S. bushel).","A vessel of the capacity of a bushel, used in measuring; a bushel measure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mend or repair clothes.","To pack grain, hops, etc. into bushel measures."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bushel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bushel","blocks":[{"text":"A bushel is an imperial and US customary unit of volume, based upon an earlier measure of dry capacity. The old bushel was used mostly for agricultural products, such as wheat: in modern usage, the volume is nominal, with bushels denoting a mass defined differently for each commodity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Queensland_Government_Imperial_Bushel_AD1875.jpg/330px-Queensland_Government_Imperial_Bushel_AD1875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bushel","wikidata":"Q216658"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Bushel_Table_of_States.jpg/500px-Bushel_Table_of_States.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Bushel_Table_of_States.jpg/1280px-Bushel_Table_of_States.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Bushel_Table_of_States.jpg/500px-Bushel_Table_of_States.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A table of weights from the secretaries of the different American states, showing the number of pounds which their laws recognize as a bushel of different articles, c. 1